# This template deliberately installs each dependency's own current
# latest (see ../setup.sh), rather than a coordinated set of versions
# that were tested together — so a peer-dependency range that's a step
# behind (e.g. a tool still declaring `peer typescript@^5.x` after
# TypeScript 6 ships) is expected occasionally, not a sign anything is
# actually broken. Push past those instead of failing the install.
#
# `force`, not `legacy-peer-deps`: legacy-peer-deps disables npm's
# peer-dependency auto-install *entirely*, which silently breaks
# packages that rely on it (e.g. @testing-library/react declares
# @testing-library/dom as a peer, not a regular dependency — with
# legacy-peer-deps it never gets installed and `screen`/etc. go
# missing with no error at install time). `force` keeps auto-install
# for peers that resolve fine and only overrides the ones that don't.
force=true
